 ## Overview
-Coverage-based Opacity Mapping is a method to convert accumulated unnormalized Gaussian weights into per-layer coverage opacities for surface compositing. It solves the problem of jagged, aliased edges and incorrect blending in surface splatting by replacing threshold-based clamping with a smooth, monotonically increasing mapping [S1, unofficial]. Unlike the single-layer ternary depth test which produces hard transitions, this method yields smooth opacity falloff against the background and gradual transitions in overlap regions [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [SDF-based Opacity Derivation via Logistic CDF](3233-sdf-based-opacity-derivation-via-logistic-cdf.md) by handling the final compositing of surface layers rather than deriving initial opacity from signed distances.
+Coverage-based Opacity Mapping is a method to convert accumulated unnormalized Gaussian weight $W_k$ into per-layer coverage opacity $\alpha_k$, replacing threshold-based clamping with a smooth, monotonically increasing mapping based on the partition-of-unity assumption [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of jagged, aliased edges and incorrect blending in surface splatting by treating each layer as a partially covering opaque surface, allowing residual transmittance to reveal deeper layers [S1, unofficial]. Unlike the single-layer ternary depth test which produces hard transitions, this method yields smooth opacity falloff against the background and gradual transitions in overlap regions [S1, unofficial]. It operates after [SDF-based Opacity Derivation via Logistic CDF](3233-sdf-based-opacity-derivation-via-logistic-cdf.md) by handling the final compositing of surface layers rather than deriving initial opacity from signed distances [G].
 
